---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHero from '../components/PageHero.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Speaking & Media" description="Speaking, media, and moderated-conversation topics from Ryan Brown and Convera Strategies." canonical="/speaking/">
  <PageHero eyebrow="Speaking · Media · Moderated Conversation" title="Ideas in public." lede="Ryan Brown speaks about institutions, law and justice, workplace culture, public authority, research, and the ways organizations create meaning through language, records, decisions, and practice.">
    <div slot="actions">
      <a class="button button--primary" href="/contact/?reason=speaking-media&source=speaking-page" data-convera-event="speaking_contact" data-convera-source="speaking-page">Invite Ryan →</a>
      <a class="button button--ghost" href="/press-kit/">Open Press Kit</a>
    </div>
    <aside slot="aside" class="speaking-note card">
      <p class="eyebrow">For organizers</p>
      <p>Include the event, audience, proposed date, format, location or virtual platform, and any honorarium or budget information available.</p>
    </aside>
  </PageHero>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow">Topics</p>
        <h2>Areas for conversation.</h2>
        <p class="lede">These are starting points rather than fixed speeches. Programs can be shaped for professional organizations, universities, public institutions, community audiences, podcasts, panels, classrooms, and moderated discussions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3 topic-grid">
        <article class="card"><h3>Institutions, legitimacy & public authority</h3><p>How organizations explain decisions, exercise authority, and communicate legitimacy when conduct or outcomes are contested.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Law, justice & legal meaning</h3><p>How legal rules operate through institutional interpretation, procedural choices, records, and the language of courts and justice institutions.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Workplace culture & institutional narratives</h3><p>How organizations describe conduct, responsibility, reliability, and expectations—and what those descriptions reveal about institutional priorities.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Research through records</h3><p>Using archival, administrative, legal, and narrative data to examine institutions without reducing complex cases to counts alone.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Public life, trust & meaning</h3><p>How public confidence, language, social expectations, and shared interpretations shape institutional relationships.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Research-to-practice conversations</h3><p>Translating careful inquiry into questions organizations can use to examine systems, communication, process, and decision-making.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>



  <section class="section speaking-gallery" aria-labelledby="speaking-gallery-title">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="speaking-gallery__heading"><div><p class="eyebrow">Public Engagement</p><h2 id="speaking-gallery-title">Conversation in institutional settings.</h2></div><p class="lede">Panels, briefings, classrooms, workshops, and informal exchanges each require a different kind of presence. The emphasis remains on careful listening, clear questions, and useful discussion.</p></div>
      <div class="speaking-gallery__grid">
